Section 3: DevOps Tools

	Intro:
		1. Trainer Mumshad Mamnanbeth talks about the numerous numerous tools and technologies which exists in DevOps jobs and emphasizes the point
			that it could be a bit daunting, since there are so many things to learn.
		2. In this section he sets out to explain each one of them through telling a story
	Process
		1. First Release
			1.0 Let's say, first i have developed an application, it works on my machine and i can access it on my machine.
			1.1 I want to share this application with the other users as well
			1.2 I can host this application on my machine but whenever i close my computer, the website also goes down
			1.3 To prevent this issue, i need to move my application to a machine/server/VM in a data center which never closes down
			1.4 Now i have an IP address and i can access to my application with it. But for convenience for me and others, i use a domain
			name which is easier to remember.
			1.5 Now that i have my own server in a data center, i can just run my application. Not so fast, we need to satisfy some conditions
			1.6 First, i need the correct runtime installed on the server which depends on the programming language.
			1.7 Second, i need to install all the libraries and packages i have used in my application's code.
			1.8 Now my laptop which i write code became my "development environment" and the server i host my application became the "production
			environment".
			1.9 Let's say my website became so popular and started to get requested a lot so as a result i need a better organized process.
		2. Basic Workflow
			2.0 At first my initial workflow was just writing the code in text, building it and then moving the executable/binary to the server.
			2.1 Code is written as text and to be able to turn this into a application which could be run we need to convert this 
			into an executable or a binary.
			2.2 To do this build tools are used
			2.3 Now this process is simple: write code, build it and deploy it to the server
		3. Scaling collaboration with Git and Github
			3.0 As the users demands more and more functionality from my website, my developer friends joins me and suddenly we are a team
			3.1 We want to develop more features for this project together and we are working on the same files at the same time.
			3.2 Each one of us puts the code we have created into a central hub but this turns into a problem since controlling and managing
			this process becomes a lot complicated.
			3.3 To solve this problem we use "git", a distributed version control system which keeps track of every file in the repository
			and provides mechanicms to get the remote repository to the local machine and vice versa. Github or GitLab may be used for storing
			remote repositories.
			3.4 The team is able to collaborate efficiently and effectively without breaking each other's code.
			3.5 As a single person developing, i used to build the app on my machine. Now we are a team we need another external entity
			to do this, so errors and mixups does not happen
		4. Introduction CI/CD for Automated Deployment
			4.1 Provisioning a dedicated build server is thus necessary.
			4.2 Since deploying the built application to the production environment without first actually testing it risky.
			4.3 We also need a "test server"
			4.4 Now the workflow becomes Develop->Github->Build server->Test server->Production server. Now moving pieces manually from one
			server to another slow and can be automated with CI/CD tools like github actions, jenkins and gitlab CI/CD.
			4.5 Remember, we could not just run the application on any server without configuring it first. Yes, we need to determine the
			exact dependencies both runtime, library and packages and install them on each one of our servers.
		5. Packaging with Containers
			5.1 Since doing this manually is both tedious and prone to errors. We need to find a solution, technology to make things easier.
			5.2 We can use container technologies like docker for this very purpose. Containers are isolated running environments which are
			easy to configure, deploy and move around.
			5.3 Developers define all the dependencies in a docker file and pushes it to Github.
			5.4 Build server pulls the file and builds the docker image from it and now every server can run containers with this image.
			5.5 Now we have our CI/CD line in check and installing dependencies for our application figured out thanks to container
			technologies.
			5.6 Now we have container at our hands and we need to manage containers on many servers to be able to meet with the demands. Doing
				this manually is just not feasible considering the scale of the operation.
			5.7 And we need a product/solution/techlonogy for automating this.
		6. Scaling up with Container Orchestration
			6.1 To be able to start new containers to meet with the demand and bring them down when unnecessary, restart the failed ones and 
			provision new ones instead we need a container orchestration technology which is kubernetes.
			6.2 With kubernetes scaling up, down and healing the containers is automated. Also kubernetes can also auto-scale the underlying
			infrastructure as well.
			6.3 Now we also have a way of maintaining and managing containers.
			6.4 But we still need to servers to be deployed exactly identical to each other. Currently this is done manually, is it possible to
			automate this with a program?
		7. Infrastructure as Code/Provisioning of Infrastructure
			7.1 Each time we require a new server; correct system resources, operating system, storage, kernel settings and software needs to be
			configured.
			7.2 Instead of doing these manually on the cloud platform GUI, we can define them as code and use a product called "TerraForm" to
			do this automatically instead
			7.3 This makes it easier for us track and change the provisioning configuration of servers.
			7.4 Now we have handled the automation, tracking, managing provisioning of the infrastructure, we need to be able to complete the
			remaining configurations after the server is up and running
		8. Configuration of Infrastructure/Post Provisioning Configuration of Infrastructure
			8.1 Provisioning the server identically, managing their templates as manifest files is half the job and the remaining is completing
			it with ansible in the same manner.
			8.2 With ansible, we can make sure the live configurations of each and every server is the same. We can keep track and manage it 
			conveniently with git as well
			8.3 Now that we have completed the whole cycle we need to be able track and monitor our operation.
		9. Monitoring Infrastructure
			9.1 We need a way to keep track of and monitor the CPU, RAM, storage i/ops and process status of our whole operation
			9.2 That is done with prometheus which collects this information and grafana which displays them.	
	
	Conclusion:

		1. We have moved together through each stage from manual task to an automated version of it, in a very high level way.
		2. In this section, i was able to understand each category of tools and reason it was created.
		3. I do think that understanding exactly why a tool was created, to address what problem comes first and makes it easier for me to
		understand the context.

